{
  "id": "py-31-split-join",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u5",
  "order": 31,
  "title": "split and join",
  "summary": "split() cuts text into a list of words, join() glues a list of strings back into one text.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "cells",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "Text and lists turn into each other all the time.\n\n**split** cuts text into a list of strings:\n\n- `\"the cat sat\".split()` cuts at spaces: `['the', 'cat', 'sat']`. Extra spaces are ignored\n- `\"4,9,2\".split(\",\")` cuts at every comma exactly: `['4', '9', '2']`\n\nThe pieces are always **strings**, even when they look like numbers. Use `int()` on a piece to do math with it.\n\n**join** does the opposite. It is called on the **separator**, and glues the items of a list together with it:\n\n```\n\"-\".join([\"up\", \"and\", \"away\"])\n```\n\ngives `'up-and-away'`. join only takes strings, so a list of numbers needs `str()` first.\n\nIn the **cells** panel the text gets red cut marks where split cuts, and the list it makes appears as a second row of boxes.",
    "analogy": "A loaf of bread and a knife. split slices the loaf wherever the marks are and drops the marks. join lays the slices in a row again, with whatever you choose in between, like butter."
  },
